#{[17592186562468 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562468 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106875" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562468 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562468 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R),$ the vector space of all $5\\times 1$ matrices over $R?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562468 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ec0dc53-4341-5ee6-84a6-d12231a31e63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562468 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nThus, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R)$ contains $5\\cdot 1=5$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R),$ we have, indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R))=5\\cdot 1=5.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562473 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562473 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562473 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562473 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${ℓ}^{2},$ the vector space of infinite real sequences where the sum of the squares of the terms are finite\n\nII. ${R}_{200}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $200$\n\nIII. ${\\text{M}}_{400}(R),$ the vector space of all $400\\times 400$ matrices over $R$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562473 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae384128-da5c-5a65-8194-114971516a6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562473 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${ℓ}^{2}$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of ${ℓ}^{2}$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n\\{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}\\},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in \\{1,2,…,n\\},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${ℓ}^{2}$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${ℓ}^{2}$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${R}_{200}[t]$ and ${\\text{M}}_{400}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{200}[t])=200+1=201,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{400}(R))=400\\cdot 400=160\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562502 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562502 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95387" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562502 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562502 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 800}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 800$ matrices over $R$\n\nII. ${R}_{500}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $500$\n\nIII. ${L}^{1}([-1,1]),$ the vector space of all functions  $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ such that ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}|f(x)|\\,\\text{d}x<\\infty $" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562502 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df717d4d-32a8-529f-8ad7-781f9e19e4d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562502 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 800}(R)$ and ${R}_{500}[t]$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{200\\times 800}(R))=200\\cdot 800=160\\,000\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{500}[t])=500+1=501,\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\n- ${L}^{1}([-1,1])$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of functions in ${L}^{1}([-1,1])$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,x,\\,{x}^{2},\\,…,{x}^{n}\\},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{x}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in [-1,1]$ if and only if ${a}_{i}=0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${L}^{1}([-1,1])$ of size $n,$ which imples that ${L}^{1}([-1,1])$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562515 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562515 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562515 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562515 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the dimension of ${R}_{13}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $13?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562515 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa31176f-d01d-551f-8a93-0788b21bb0df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562515 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{13}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{13}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{13}[t])=13+1=14.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
